          <h2 style="font-size: var(--fs-56); margin-bottom: var(--space-6);">Everything you need to know about a 10-yard dumpster.</h2>

          <h3>What is a 10-yard roll-off dumpster?</h3>
          <p>A 10-yard roll-off dumpster is the smallest container we deliver. It measures roughly <strong>12 feet long, 8 feet wide, and 3.5 feet tall</strong>, with an open top and a swing-out door on the back end so you can walk debris in instead of throwing it over the side. The "10 yard" name comes from cubic-yard volume: ten cubic yards of debris fits inside, which works out to about three full-size pickup truck loads.</p>
          <p>Roll-offs got the name because the dumpster <em>rolls off</em> the back of the delivery truck onto your driveway, then rolls back on when it's time to haul. There's no hopper, no compaction, no fixed dumpster on wheels — it's a steel container that gets set, filled, and pulled. Simple and predictable.</p>

          <h3>Common 10-yard projects in the DMV</h3>
          <p>The 10-yard is the right call when you've got a contained, one-room or single-zone cleanout. After two decades of drops across Prince George's and Montgomery County, here's where we see it shine:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Garage and basement cleanouts.</strong> Stored boxes, broken tools, holiday decor, empty paint cans, leftover lumber. A 10-yard handles a two-car garage's worth of accumulated stuff.</li>
            <li><strong>Single-bathroom remodels.</strong> Tear out a cast-iron tub, vanity, tile, drywall, and a toilet and you'll fit inside a 10-yard with room left over. Most full-bath demos clock in around 5–7 cubic yards.</li>
            <li><strong>Yard waste and tree removal.</strong> Branches, brush, sod, mulch, leaves, a small uprooted tree. Yard waste is bulky but light, so volume hits before weight.</li>
            <li><strong>Estate downsizing or moving cleanouts.</strong> One bedroom or a small apartment's worth of furniture, mattresses, clothes, and household goods.</li>
            <li><strong>Small flooring or kitchen-tile rip-ups.</strong> If you're only pulling tile or hardwood from one room, a 10 is usually enough — unless the tile is thick or set on concrete board.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>How much weight can a 10-yard hold?</h3>
          <p>Every 10-yard rental from JP Roll-Off includes a <strong>1-ton (2,000 lb) weight allowance</strong> built into the flat rate. For perspective: a fully-loaded car-axle scale ticket on a typical garage cleanout runs 700–1,200 pounds. Most residential jobs stay comfortably inside the tonnage limit, which is why we built that allowance into the price instead of charging it separately.</p>
          <p>What fills the weight before the volume? Concrete, dirt, brick, roofing shingles, and plaster lath. If your job is mostly heavy materials, ask about our flat-rate <a href="/services/15-yard-dumpster">15-yard heavy-debris quote</a> instead — you'll often pay less by going bigger on weight allowance.</p>

          <h3>Where will the 10-yard fit on my property?</h3>
          <p>This is the size people pick when driveway space is tight. A 10-yard fits in a single car spot — about the footprint of a midsize sedan — so you can keep the second half of the driveway clear for daily life. Standard placement options:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Driveway, parallel to the house.</strong> Most common. We place 2×10 protective boards under the rails so there's no scarring on asphalt or concrete.</li>
            <li><strong>Driveway, perpendicular to the curb.</strong> Useful when you want to pull the door-end toward the work area for walk-in loading.</li>
            <li><strong>Side yard or alleyway.</strong> If we can swing the truck in cleanly. Send a quick photo when you book and we'll spec it.</li>
            <li><strong>On the street (with permit).</strong> If your house has no driveway access, your jurisdiction can issue a temporary right-of-way permit. We'll walk you through it.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
